::ALG2_U02_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = 2x<sup>4</sup> - 1? {
=both ends rise #Correct. The leading term 2x<sup>4</sup> has even degree and positive coefficient.
~both ends fall #That would require an even degree with a negative leading coefficient.
~left falls, right rises #That is odd positive behavior.
~left rises, right falls #That is odd negative behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = -3x<sup>5</sup> + x? {
~both ends rise #Odd-degree functions have opposite end behavior.
=left rises, right falls #Correct. Odd degree with a negative leading coefficient rises left and falls right.
~left falls, right rises #That would match a positive leading coefficient.
~both ends fall #Both ends falling is even negative behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which term controls end behavior of 4x<sup>6</sup> - 9x<sup>2</sup> + 3? {
~-9x<sup>2</sup> #This is not the highest-degree term.
~3 #A constant does not determine long-run behavior.
=4x<sup>6</sup> #Correct. The highest-degree term dominates for very large positive or negative x-values.
~all terms equally #The leading term dominates the lower-degree terms.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of f(x) = -7x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 1? {
~2 #The polynomial includes a cubic term.
~-7 #This is the leading coefficient.
~1 #The constant term does not make the polynomial degree 1.
=3 #Correct. The greatest exponent in the polynomial is 3.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the leading coefficient of f(x) = -7x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 1? {
=-7 #Correct. The coefficient on the highest-degree term is -7.
~3 #This is the degree.
~2 #This coefficient belongs to a lower-degree term.
~-1 #This is the constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which end behavior matches g(x) = -5x<sup>4</sup> + 8x? {
~both ends rise #A negative leading coefficient on an even degree makes both ends fall.
=both ends fall #Correct. The leading term is even degree and negative.
~left falls, right rises #That is odd positive behavior.
~left rises, right falls #That is odd negative behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>For h(x) = x<sup>7</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup>, what happens as x increases without bound? {
~h(x) decreases without bound #A positive odd-degree leading term rises to the right.
~h(x) approaches 0 #This polynomial does not approach 0 on the right.
=h(x) increases without bound #Correct. The leading term x<sup>7</sup> controls the right end and is positive.
~h(x) stays constant #The output grows without bound.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>For p(x) = -2x<sup>6</sup> + 11, what happens as x decreases without bound? {
~p(x) increases without bound #A negative even-degree leading term falls on the left.
~p(x) approaches 11 #The constant does not control far-left behavior.
~p(x) approaches 0 #The leading term grows in magnitude.
=p(x) decreases without bound #Correct. Even degree with a negative leading coefficient falls on both ends.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which polynomial has left end down and right end up? {
=3x<sup>5</sup> - x #Correct. Odd degree with a positive leading coefficient falls left and rises right.
~-x<sup>4</sup> + 2 #Even degree gives the same direction on both ends.
~x<sup>4</sup> - 2x #Even degree with positive leading coefficient rises on both ends.
~-3x<sup>5</sup> + x #Odd degree with a negative coefficient rises left and falls right.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which polynomial has both ends falling? {
~4x<sup>2</sup> - 9 #Even degree with positive leading coefficient rises on both ends.
=-2x<sup>8</sup> + x #Correct. Even degree with a negative leading coefficient falls on both ends.
~5x<sup>3</sup> - 1 #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
~-x<sup>5</sup> + 6 #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the leading term of f(x) = 6 - 3x + 9x<sup>4</sup>? {
~6 #This is the constant term.
~-3x #This is a lower-degree term.
=9x<sup>4</sup> #Correct. The highest-degree term is 9x<sup>4</sup>.
~x<sup>4</sup> #This omits the coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is true for f(x) = -x<sup>9</sup> + 2x<sup>3</sup> - 4? {
~The graph falls left and rises right. #That would require a positive leading coefficient.
~Both ends rise. #That requires even degree with positive leading coefficient.
~Both ends fall. #That requires even degree with negative leading coefficient.
=The graph rises left and falls right. #Correct. The leading term is odd degree with a negative coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which term determines the end behavior of 12x - x<sup>5</sup> + 4x<sup>2</sup>? {
=-x<sup>5</sup> #Correct. The fifth-degree term controls end behavior.
~12x #This is not the highest-degree term.
~4x<sup>2</sup> #This term is lower degree.
~12x + 4x<sup>2</sup> #End behavior is controlled by the highest-degree term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of f(x) = 10 - 4x<sup>8</sup> + x<sup>3</sup>? {
~10 #This is the constant term.
=8 #Correct. The greatest exponent is 8.
~4 #The coefficient is not the degree.
~3 #There is an eighth-degree term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the leading coefficient of q(x) = 2x<sup>2</sup> - 9x<sup>6</sup> + 5? {
~2 #This coefficient is on a lower-degree term.
~6 #This is the degree.
=-9 #Correct. The leading term is -9x<sup>6</sup>.
~5 #This is the constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial falls left and rises right. What must be true about its leading term? {
~It has odd degree and negative coefficient. #That would rise left and fall right.
~It has even degree and positive coefficient. #That would rise on both ends.
~It has even degree and negative coefficient. #That would fall on both ends.
=It has odd degree and positive coefficient. #Correct. Positive odd-degree leading terms fall left and rise right.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial rises on both ends. Which leading term could it have? {
=7x<sup>8</sup> #Correct. Positive even-degree leading terms rise on both ends.
~-4x<sup>6</sup> #Negative even-degree leading terms fall on both ends.
~3x<sup>5</sup> #Odd degree gives opposite end behavior.
~-x<sup>7</sup> #Odd degree gives opposite end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which describes f(x) = 0.5x<sup>3</sup> - 2x<sup>2</sup> + 1? {
~both ends rise #A cubic has opposite end behavior.
=left falls, right rises #Correct. The leading term is odd degree with a positive coefficient.
~both ends fall #A cubic has opposite end behavior.
~left rises, right falls #That would require a negative leading coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = -6x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 4? {
~both ends rise #A negative quadratic falls on both ends.
~left falls, right rises #That describes positive odd-degree behavior.
=both ends fall #Correct. The leading term has even degree and a negative coefficient.
~left rises, right falls #That describes negative odd-degree behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which polynomial has the same end behavior as f(x) = -x<sup>4</sup> + 7? {
~3x<sup>6</sup> - 2 #This rises on both ends.
~-3x<sup>5</sup> + 2 #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
~3x<sup>5</sup> - 2 #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
=-3x<sup>6</sup> + 2 #Correct. Both have even degree and negative leading coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = 8x<sup>10</sup> - x<sup>9</sup>, which part matters most for end behavior? {
=8x<sup>10</sup>, because it has the greatest degree #Correct. Degree 10 is greater than degree 9.
~-x<sup>9</sup>, because it is negative #Sign matters after identifying the highest degree.
~both terms equally #The highest-degree term dominates.
~the constant term #There is no constant term controlling end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>A graph rises left and falls right. Which function could match? {
~f(x) = x<sup>4</sup> - 1 #This rises on both ends.
=f(x) = -x<sup>3</sup> + 1 #Correct. A negative cubic rises left and falls right.
~f(x) = -x<sup>4</sup> + 1 #This falls on both ends.
~f(x) = x<sup>3</sup> - 1 #This falls left and rises right.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is the leading term of r(x) = -2 + 5x<sup>2</sup> - 3x<sup>7</sup>? {
~5x<sup>2</sup> #This is a lower-degree term.
~-2 #This is the constant term.
=-3x<sup>7</sup> #Correct. It has the greatest exponent.
~7 #This is the degree, not a term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x) has leading term -0.25x<sup>12</sup>, what happens to both ends? {
~both ends rise #The coefficient is negative.
~left falls, right rises #Odd positive behavior does not apply.
~left rises, right falls #Odd negative behavior does not apply.
=both ends fall #Correct. Even degree and negative leading coefficient means both ends fall.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>If a polynomial has odd degree and positive leading coefficient, which statement is true? {
=The left end falls and the right end rises. #Correct. Positive odd degree falls left and rises right.
~Both ends rise. #That describes positive even degree.
~Both ends fall. #That describes negative even degree.
~The left end rises and the right end falls. #That describes negative odd degree.
}

